const mindMap = `
# Tool Design & Structured Output
## Why It Matters
### LLM sees only name, description, schema
### Description = LLM contract
### Max 3–5 tools per agent
## Good Descriptions
### What / When / Returns
### Verb-noun naming
### Disambiguate — Do NOT use for X
## Input Schema
### Per-property descriptions
### enum over open string
### required = mandatory only
## Structured Output
### Prompt JSON — unreliable
### Forced tool use — guaranteed
### No JSON.parse needed
## Zod Validation
### Single schema source
### TypeScript inference
### Return errors to LLM
## Tensions
### Fine-grained vs coarse-grained
### Strict schema vs change cost
### Return errors vs raise exceptions
### MCP reuse vs latency overhead
## Pitfalls
### Missing property descriptions
### string instead of enum
### Too many tools degrades selection
### Prompt-based JSON output
`;
---

<LessonLayout title="L3 — Tool Design & Structured Output"
>

  <LessonHeader
    num="L03"
    title="Tool Design & Structured Output"
    subtitle="The LLM reads your descriptions, not you. Write them well and the agent becomes reliable."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Why tool design matters</h2>

    <p>
      In Lesson 2 you gave the LLM a <code>run_shell</code> tool and it worked. But that was a toy.
      In a real agent — one that talks to a database, sends emails, updates records — the LLM must
      choose the <em>right</em> tool, with the <em>right</em> arguments, every time.
    </p>
    <p>The LLM makes that choice by reading three things:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The tool's <strong>name</strong></li>
      <li>The tool's <strong>description</strong></li>
      <li>The tool's <strong>input schema</strong> (JSON Schema object)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      That is all it sees. Your implementation is invisible. Which means <strong>the description
      is your contract with the LLM</strong> — it determines when the tool gets called and how
      arguments are filled.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Writing good tool descriptions</h2>

    <p>A description must answer three questions the LLM silently asks before calling a tool:</p>
    <ol class="tool-desc-list">
      <li><em>What does this tool do?</em></li>
      <li><em>When should I use it (vs the other tools)?</em></li>
      <li><em>What does it return?</em></li>
    </ol>

    <p>Compare these two descriptions for a database lookup tool:</p>

    <CodeBlock lang="text" label="bad — don't do this" code={`'Look up user data'`} />

    <CodeBlock lang="text" label="good" code={`
'Look up a user record by their ID or email.
Returns { id, name, email, role, createdAt }.
Use this when you need to verify who a user is or retrieve their account details.
Do NOT use this for searching — use search_users instead.'
    `} />

    <p>
      The good version tells the LLM <em>what it returns</em>, when to prefer it, and explicitly
      rules out a confusion case. That last sentence — "Do NOT use this for searching" — is
      especially important when you have two similar tools.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="3–5 tools maximum per agent">
      <p>
        Each additional tool makes the LLM's decision harder and increases wrong-tool selection
        risk. Above 7–10 tools, selection accuracy degrades noticeably. If you need more, split
        into multiple agents with focused tool sets.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Anatomy of a good input schema</h2>

    <p>
      The <code>input_schema</code> is a JSON Schema object. Every property should have a type
      <em>and</em> a description. The LLM reads property descriptions to know what value to pass.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="send-notification tool" code={`
const sendNotificationTool = {
  name: 'send_notification',
  description:
    'Send a push notification to a user. Use when the task requires alerting a specific user. ' +
    'Returns { success: boolean, messageId: string }. ' +
    'Do NOT use for email — use send_email instead.',
  input_schema: {
    type: 'object',
    properties: {
      userId: {
        type: 'string',
        description: 'The UUID of the user to notify',
      },
      message: {
        type: 'string',
        description: 'The notification body. Plain text, max 140 characters.',
      },
      priority: {
        type: 'string',
        enum: ['low', 'normal', 'high'],
        description: "Urgency level. Use 'high' only for time-sensitive alerts.",
      },
    },
    required: ['userId', 'message'], // priority is optional — defaults to 'normal'
  },
};
    `} />

    <p>Notice:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Every property has a <code>description</code> — not just the tool itself</li>
      <li><code>enum</code> constrains the LLM to valid values, preventing hallucinated strings like <code>"urgent"</code></li>
      <li>Optional fields stay out of <code>required</code></li>
      <li>The tool description says what it returns</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Structured output — forced tool use</h2>

    <p>
      Sometimes you don't want tool calls — you want the LLM to produce a <em>typed object</em>
      as its final answer. Examples: extract entities from text, classify a support ticket,
      triage a request.
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Method</th><th>How</th><th>Reliability</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Prompt-based</strong></td>
            <td>Tell the LLM "respond only with JSON" in the system prompt</td>
            <td>Low — it sometimes adds prose anyway</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Forced tool use</strong></td>
            <td>Define a <code>respond</code> tool and force the LLM to call it via <code>tool_choice</code></td>
            <td>High — tool calls always produce schema-conforming JSON</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>Here is the forced tool use pattern — extracting a typed task from natural language:</p>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="extract-task.ts" code={`
import Anthropic from '@anthropic-ai/sdk';

const client = new Anthropic({ apiKey: process.env.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY });

// The output schema, disguised as a tool
const respondTool = {
  name: 'respond',
  description: 'Return the extracted task as structured data',
  input_schema: {
    type: 'object',
    properties: {
      title: {
        type: 'string',
        description: 'Short task title, max 8 words',
      },
      description: {
        type: 'string',
        description: 'Full task description in one sentence',
      },
      priority: {
        type: 'string',
        enum: ['low', 'medium', 'high'],
        description: "Inferred urgency from the user's language",
      },
      dueDate: {
        type: 'string',
        description: 'ISO 8601 date if mentioned, otherwise null',
        nullable: true,
      },
    },
    required: ['title', 'description', 'priority'],
  },
};

async function extractTask(userInput: string) {
  const response = await client.messages.create({
    model: 'claude-haiku-4-5-20251001',
    max_tokens: 512,
    system: "You are a task extraction assistant. Extract the task from the user's message.",
    tools: [respondTool],
    tool_choice: { type: 'tool', name: 'respond' }, // ← forces the LLM to call this tool
    messages: [{ role: 'user', content: userInput }],
  });

  // The tool call input IS the structured output — no JSON.parse needed
  const toolCall = response.content.find(b => b.type === 'tool_use');
  return toolCall.input; // { title, description, priority, dueDate }
}

const result = await extractTask(
  "Hey, can you remind me to send the Q3 report to Sarah by Friday? It's pretty urgent."
);

console.log(JSON.stringify(result, null, 2));
// {
//   "title": "Send Q3 report to Sarah",
//   "description": "Send the Q3 report to Sarah by Friday as it is urgent.",
//   "priority": "high",
//   "dueDate": "2026-06-20"
// }
    `} />

    <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="No JSON.parse needed">
      <p>
        Tool calls always produce schema-conforming input — guaranteed by the API.
        No try-catch around <code>JSON.parse</code>, no regex, no prompt retries.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Validation with Zod (TypeScript equivalent of Pydantic)</h2>

    <p>
      The API guarantees the shape matches your schema, but doesn't give you TypeScript types.
      Use <strong>Zod</strong> to validate and get full type inference:
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="bash" label="terminal" code={`npm install zod`} />

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="validate with zod" code={`
import { z } from 'zod';

// Define the shape once with Zod
const TaskSchema = z.object({
  title:       z.string().max(60),
  description: z.string(),
  priority:    z.enum(['low', 'medium', 'high']),
  dueDate:     z.string().nullable(),
});

type Task = z.infer<typeof TaskSchema>;

// After getting toolCall.input from the API:
const task: Task = TaskSchema.parse(toolCall.input);
// task is now fully typed — autocomplete works, type errors caught at compile time
    `} />

    <p>
      In a NestJS service, you'd use a Zod schema (or class-validator DTO) to validate the
      output before passing it downstream — exactly the same pattern as validating a request body.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Tool design checklist</h2>

    <LessonChecklist
      title="Before adding a tool to an agent, verify:"
      items={[
        '<strong>Name is a clear verb-noun</strong> — <code>send_email</code> not <code>email</code> or <code>mailer</code>',
        '<strong>Description says what it returns</strong> — the LLM needs to know what data it gets back',
        '<strong>Description says when <em>not</em> to use it</strong> — prevents confusion with similar tools',
        '<strong>Every property has a description</strong> — the LLM reads these to decide argument values',
        '<strong>Enums used for finite valid values</strong> — prevents hallucinated strings like <code>"urgent"</code>',
        '<strong>Optional fields excluded from <code>required</code></strong> — LLM won\'t be forced to invent values',
        '<strong>Total tools per agent ≤ 5</strong> — more tools = slower + less reliable selection',
      ]}
    />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Quick-check quiz</h2>

    <LessonQuiz questions={[
      {
        q: 'You have get_user and search_users. What should the get_user description explicitly include?',
        options: [
          'A note that says "Do NOT use this for searching — use search_users instead"',
          'The implementation details of how it queries the database',
          'The name of the developer who wrote it',
          'Nothing extra — the name is self-explanatory',
        ],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'When two tools are similar, the LLM needs explicit disambiguation. "Do NOT use this for searching" directly rules out the confusion case.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Why use enum in a tool input schema for a field like priority?',
        options: [
          'To prevent the LLM from passing invalid strings like "urgent" or "ASAP"',
          'To make the JSON schema file smaller',
          'Enums are required by the Anthropic API',
          'To improve performance by reducing token count',
        ],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'Without enum, the LLM can pass any string — "urgent", "critical", "high priority". Enum constrains the LLM to values your code actually handles.',
      },
      {
        q: 'What is the "forced tool use" pattern for structured output?',
        options: [
          'Define a "respond" tool with your output schema and pass tool_choice: { type: "tool", name: "respond" }',
          'Telling the LLM "only respond with JSON" in the system prompt',
          'Using JSON.parse on the LLM\'s text output',
          'Sending the output to a second LLM to reformat it',
        ],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'Forced tool use makes the LLM produce schema-conforming JSON via the tool call mechanism — more reliable than prompt instructions which the LLM sometimes ignores.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Why keep tool count to 3–5 per agent?',
        options: [
          'More tools increase decision complexity, raising wrong-tool selection risk',
          'The Anthropic API hard-limits to 5 tools per request',
          'Each tool adds $0.01 to the API cost',
          'More than 5 tools causes the agent loop to break',
        ],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'Above 7–10 tools, LLM tool selection accuracy degrades. The LLM starts confusing similar tools. This is a hard architectural constraint — if you need more, use multiple agents.',
      },
    ]} />

  </div>

  <DeepDive
    sources="l1_1.6, l2_2.2, l2_2.3"
    tensions={[
      { a: 'Fine-grained tools (control)', b: 'Coarse-grained tools (fewer LLM decisions)' },
      { a: 'Strict schema validation',     b: 'Deployment coordination when values change' },
      { a: 'Tool reliability / error returns', b: 'Explicit failures (raise exceptions)' },
      { a: 'MCP interoperability',         b: 'Direct integration (lower latency overhead)' },
    ]}
    questions={[
      {
        q:      'An LLM keeps calling the wrong tool for a specific query pattern. Walk through the systematic debugging process.',
        hint:   'Wrong tool selection is usually a description problem or a tool similarity problem. Diagnose before prescribing.',
        answer: '<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Log the full context sent to the LLM when the wrong tool is selected — what did the model see?</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Check if the two tools have similar descriptions. If so, make descriptions more discriminative: add "use this when X, NOT when Y."</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Check if the query pattern contains words from the wrong tool\'s description. Update the right tool\'s description to address this pattern explicitly.</p><p><strong>Step 4:</strong> If the model consistently misroutes, add a few-shot example to the system prompt showing the exact query → correct tool mapping.</p><p><strong>Step 5:</strong> If the problem persists, add a routing pre-processor: a cheap LLM call that classifies query intent and filters which tools are available for this query type.</p>',
      },
      {
        q:      'Design a tool permission system where different users can invoke different subsets of tools. How does this interact with agent decision-making?',
        hint:   'Permissions live at the tool layer, not the prompt layer. The LLM should never know about tools it cannot use.',
        answer: '<p>At request time: load the user\'s permission set, filter the full tool list to only permitted tools, pass only permitted tools to <code>messages.create()</code>.</p><p>The LLM never sees tools it cannot use — there is no prompt injection path to access forbidden tools because the schema simply isn\'t in context.</p><p>Additionally: validate at the tool execution layer that the requesting user has permission — defence in depth in case tool schema filtering has a bug. Two independent gates.</p>',
      },
      {
        q:      'How do you version tool APIs so that changes don\'t break agents in production?',
        hint:   'Tool APIs are contracts between the LLM and the implementation. Treat them like any other API contract.',
        answer: '<p><strong>Additive changes</strong> (new optional fields) are safe. <strong>Removing or renaming</strong> parameters is a breaking change — requires versioning (<code>search_inventory_v2</code>).</p><p>Never change a tool\'s parameter names without a migration — the LLM has learned to call the tool with specific names from the description.</p><p>Deprecate, don\'t delete: run old and new versions side by side, with the old version logging a warning when called. Run LLM regression tests after any change: does the agent still call the tool correctly for the 20 most common query patterns?</p><p>Treat the docstring as part of the API contract — changes to the description can change LLM behaviour even if the code is unchanged.</p>',
      },
      {
        q:      'Design a structured output schema for a classifier that needs to handle documents that don\'t fit any known category.',
        hint:   'Closed-world schemas force the LLM to pick a wrong answer rather than admitting uncertainty.',
        answer: '<p>Add an explicit escape hatch: <code>category: enum[\'billing\', \'technical\', \'refund\', \'other\']</code>.</p><p>When <code>other</code> is selected, require: <code>reasoning: string</code> (why no category fits), <code>suggested_category?: string</code> (what new category would fit), <code>confidence: enum[\'high\', \'medium\', \'low\']</code>.</p><p>The application layer handles "other" by routing to human review and logging for analysis — the "other" distribution tells you what new categories to add.</p><p><strong>Never ship a classifier schema without an "other" option</strong> — unknown inputs are a certainty in production.</p>',
      },
    ]}
    pitfalls={[
      'Omitting property descriptions in input schemas — the LLM reads property descriptions to decide argument values. Without them, it guesses based on the property name alone.',
      'Using a finite <code>string</code> field instead of <code>enum</code> for values like priority or status — the LLM will pass creative alternatives ("urgent", "ASAP", "critical") that your switch statement doesn\'t handle.',
      'Over-exposing tools — providing 10+ tools to a single agent thinking it makes the agent more capable. Above 7–10 tools, selection accuracy degrades; split into specialist sub-agents with focused tool sets.',
      'Relying on prompt-based JSON requests ("respond only with JSON") — the LLM sometimes adds prose regardless. Forced tool use via <code>tool_choice</code> is the only reliable structured output mechanism.',
    ]}
  />

  <LessonMindMap content={mindMap} />

</LessonLayout>

